{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-06-price-changes-break-even-unknowns",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Changed Selling Prices and Unknown Cost or Margin",
    "description": "Use two possible sale prices or outcomes to determine the original cost, break-even price, or required profit.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Translate paired sale conditions into equations using a shared CP: changing SP changes the gain/loss by the same currency amount, while a percentage change is still based on CP. Solve for CP, a target SP, or an unknown percentage when two possible outcomes are stated. Include break-even, equal-rupee gain/loss comparisons, and a validation by substituting the solved cost into both conditions. Use solved examples 8, 24, 25, and 31 and similar objective patterns."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Changed Selling Prices and Unknown Cost or Margin: Use two possible sale prices or outcomes to determine the original cost, break-even price, or required profit. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When the same item has two possible selling prices, its CP is unchanged. The difference between the receipts is therefore the difference between the profit/loss amounts.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If one sale creates a 20% loss and another a 5% gain, the spread is 25% of CP. If the two sale prices differ by ₹500, then 0.25CP=500 and CP=₹2,000.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The equation works because both rates share the same CP. The cost is the common denominator even though the sale amounts change.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a break-even sale, SP=CP. If a seller currently loses 12%, then current SP is 88% of CP; the extra amount needed to break even is 12% of CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If a sale price increases by a fixed amount and changes a loss into a gain, set the gap equal to the sum of those two percentage portions of CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: a 20% loss becomes a 5% gain after adding ₹100 to SP. The change is 25% of CP, so CP=100/0.25=₹400.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When an unknown percentage is stated through a rupee difference, write the SP expressions explicitly. Avoid combining rates until you have confirmed they use the same CP base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the question asks for a new selling price that produces a target gain, calculate CP from the original condition, then multiply CP by 1+target rate.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A common shortcut uses ratios: for identical CP, SP at a loss of l% and SP at gain g% are in ratio (100−l):(100+g). Derive this from the common cost rather than memorizing it without context.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If a profit amount equals a loss amount at two different prices, their midpoint is CP. Equal currency distances on either side make the average of the two SPs the cost.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If one sale gives profit ₹p and another loss ₹p, CP is the average of those two selling prices. Check the equal differences before using the midpoint shortcut.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A given profit percentage on SP is not the standard profit percentage on CP. Convert it to an equation: profit=r%×SP, while CP=SP−profit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "After solving, substitute CP back into each sale condition. Both predicted outcomes must match the stated prices or amounts.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Keep exact fractions when a percentage is fractional. For instance, 12.5% is 1/8, and a 12.5%-to-17.5% spread is exactly 30% of CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A sensible result is positive CP and lies consistently between sale prices when one is a loss sale and the other a gain sale.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
